@@ -214,6 +214,22 @@ rather than pretending it still needs a builder. Reopening it (`iterate-to-Do`)
 archives the split marker and re-enables the real Do+Check band, same as any
 other close disposition.
 
+`--accept` also writes a machine-readable **lineage** record, `split-lineage.json`,
+into every bundle it touches — the parseable inverse of the "Child slice of
+#N" breadcrumb that otherwise lives only in the filed tracker issue's body.
+Each child gets `{"version": 1, "id", "parent", "siblings", "depth"}` (its own
+id, the parent's id, the *other* children of the same split, and the parent's
+own recorded depth + 1); the parent gets `{"version": 1, "id", "children"}`
+merged into whatever it already carried. The merge matters for the mixed-role
+case: a parent that is itself a split child keeps its own `parent` /
+`siblings` / `depth` edges and simply gains `children` — one file, independent
+optional edges, no `role` discriminator, since a bundle can legitimately be
+both a child and a parent at once. The record is read back only by
+`split.read_lineage`, a tolerant module-level reader that returns `None` for
+an absent, unreadable, malformed, or wrong-version file rather than raising.
+It is deliberately outside `state.DOWNSTREAM_OF_BRIEF`: it is provenance about
+the split, not this attempt's output, so `iterate-plan` never archives it away.
+
